From: haiku-rpi
Subject: [PATCH] net: bcmgenet: delay the transmit clock in the MAC for rgmii-rxid

With phy-mode "rgmii-rxid" (the Pi 4 device tree) the PHY delays only the
receive clock, and the Broadcom PHY driver turns its transmit delay off, but
the MAC's transmit delay was disabled as well (ID_MODE_DIS): nothing delayed
the transmit clock, and at 1000 Mbit/s no frame reached the link partner
(BOOTP broadcasts never arrived). Disable it only for "rgmii", as Linux does.
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/bcmgenet.c b/drivers/net/bcmgenet.c
index a0264dc3..8600a39a 100644
--- a/drivers/net/bcmgenet.c
+++ b/drivers/net/bcmgenet.c
@@ -461,9 +461,15 @@ static int bcmgenet_adjust_link(struct bcmgenet_eth_priv *priv)
 	clrsetbits_32(priv->mac_reg + EXT_RGMII_OOB_CTRL, OOB_DISABLE,
 			RGMII_LINK | RGMII_MODE_EN);
 
-	if (phy_dev->interface == PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_RGMII ||
-	    phy_dev->interface == PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_RGMII_RXID)
+	/*
+	 * The MAC delays the transmit clock unless the PHY does: only with
+	 * "rgmii" (the PHY delays both clocks) is that delay disabled, as in
+	 * Linux. With "rgmii-rxid" the PHY driver turns its transmit delay off.
+	 */
+	if (phy_dev->interface == PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_RGMII)
 		setbits_32(priv->mac_reg + EXT_RGMII_OOB_CTRL, ID_MODE_DIS);
+	else
+		clrbits_32(priv->mac_reg + EXT_RGMII_OOB_CTRL, ID_MODE_DIS);
 
 	writel(speed << CMD_SPEED_SHIFT, (priv->mac_reg + UMAC_CMD));
 
